WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.920
How exactly do you slash your air freight costs by 30%

2
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.840
without aggressive haggling?

3
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.400
Well, it's not what you think.

4
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:08.400
The secret?

5
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.440
We didn't negotiate harder.

6
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.000
We literally just stopped splitting our own leverage

7
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.440
seven ways.

8
00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.960
Here was the bleeding neck situation.

9
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.440
We had seven totally disconnected 3PL and freight

10
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.320
partners operating on the China, Tamina, and GCC lanes.

11
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:25.600
Basically, every single one of them

12
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.880
got a slice of the pie that was just way too small

13
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.440
to earn us a real rate.

14
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.360
And absolutely nobody owned a service problem end to end.

15
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.080
It was a mess.

16
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.480
So move one, consolidate.

17
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.680
We cut that network from seven vendors straight down to three.

18
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.240
Suddenly, the volume per partner triples.

19
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.800
The rates actually move and accountability.

20
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.320
It finally has a name attached to it.

21
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.120
Move two is the mode shift.

22
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.760
Let's be real, most air freight is not actually urgent.

23
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.160
It's just late.

24
00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.520
By fixing the planning horizon, a massive share

25
00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.240
of that supposedly urgent freight

26
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.440
shifted over to planned sea freight

27
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.600
without touching service at all.

28
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.000
Then comes move three.

29
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.080
And this is crucial, KPI governance.

30
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.960
We brought in vendor scorecards with real actual consequences.

31
00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:13.600
If your on-time delivery was poor,

32
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.600
that directly cut into your next quarters volume allocation.

33
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.240
No excuses.

34
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.920
And the result of all this?

35
00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.560
A massive 30% drop in air freight costs

36
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.200
against the pre-consolidation run rate.

37
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.600
But wait, because cutting vendors actually

38
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.520
improved our end-to-end service.

39
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.800
On-time delivery jumped by 25% relatively.

40
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.000
And customs documentation accuracy

41
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.440
held flawlessly at 98% across Dubai trade and the ERP.

42
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.680
Here is the absolute biggest takeaway from all of this.

43
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.360
Consolidation is a planning decision

44
00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.800
before it is ever a procurement decision.

45
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.920
Your real leverage starts right there

46
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.960
with operational planning.

47
00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.120
This whole operating system was engineered

48
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.200
by the NIAC Bidani, a demand-planning and AI

49
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.120
specialist based in Dubai.

50
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.720
So I'll leave you with this.

51
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.440
What piece of your supply chain is

52
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.880
splitting its own leverage right now?
